# Gurgaon Real Estate Market Analysis

Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) of ~19,500 residential property listings across Gurgaon
(Gurugram), India — built to help a real-estate advisory firm answer data-backed questions
for buyers, investors, and developers.

## Business Questions Answered

1. Which is the costliest flat in the dataset?
2. Which locality has the highest average price?
3. Which locality has the highest rate per square foot?
4. Do ready-to-move properties cost more than under-construction properties?
5. Do RERA-approved properties command a price premium?
6. How does area (sqft) impact property price?
7. Which BHK configuration is the most expensive on average?
8. Which property type is the costliest?
9. Do certain builders or companies consistently price higher?
10. Are larger homes always more expensive per square foot?

## Key Findings

- The costliest listing is a 6 BHK apartment in **DLF Camellias, Sector 42** — **₹122.63 crore**.
- **Baliawas** and **Sector 42** have the highest average prices; **Sector 42** and **Sector 113**
  have the highest average rate per sqft.
- **RERA-approved** properties carry a modest **~5% price premium** by median price.
- **Apartments** account for the largest share of total market value versus Plots, Floors,
  Villas, Houses, and Penthouses.
- Larger homes are **not** consistently more expensive per sqft — small luxury units can carry
  a higher premium than large plots.

Full analysis, charts, and caveats are in the notebook.

## Repository Contents

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `Gurugram_Real_Estate_Analysis.ipynb` | Full EDA notebook — cleaning, analysis, and insights |
| `requirements.txt` | Python dependencies |
| `Gurgaon_Real_Estate_Presentation.pptx` | Slide summary for stakeholder presentation |

> **Note:** The dataset (`Gurugram.csv`) is not included in this repo. Download it from
> [Kaggle](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/nikhilmehrahr26/gurgaon-real-estate-dataset) and
> place it in the project root before running the notebook.

## Dataset

- **Source:** [Kaggle — Gurgaon Real Estate Dataset](https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/nikhilmehrahr26/gurgaon-real-estate-dataset)
- **Size:** 19,515 listings × 12 columns
- **Columns:** Price, Status, Area, Rate per sqft, Property Type, Locality, Builder Name,
  RERA Approval, BHK_Count, Society, Company Name, Flat Type

## Methodology

1. **Data loading & understanding** — inspect shape, dtypes, and summary statistics.
2. **Cleaning** — standardize column names, convert price/area/rate fields to numeric,
   normalize categorical text, drop duplicates.
3. **Analysis** — group-by and aggregation (mean/median/sum) per business question, plus
   scatter plots for area-vs-price and area-vs-rate relationships.
4. **Insights** — each question is answered with the actual numbers, alongside caveats where
   the data has quality issues (e.g. inconsistent builder-name spellings, outlier-driven means).

## How to Run

```bash
git clone <this-repo-url>
cd <this-repo>
pip install -r requirements.txt
jupyter notebook Gurugram_Real_Estate_Analysis.ipynb
```

## Tools Used

`pandas` · `numpy` · `matplotlib` · `seaborn` · `plotly` · Jupyter Notebook

## Limitations

- `builder_name` / `company_name` contain inconsistent and duplicated text (typos, generic
  phrases) and should be cleaned before builder-level insights are used for decisions.
- `property_type` is a free-text listing title rather than a clean category; `flat_type` is
  the more reliable field for category-level comparisons.
- Several figures (e.g. top BHK configurations, top localities) are influenced by a small
  number of ultra-luxury outliers.

